Vision Language Models (VLMs) are foundational for low-latency, privacy-preserving on-device AI in real-time applications like UI agents and VQA. The VLM prefilling phase, which processes the entire visual-textual input, faces the critical challenge of a long Time-to-First-Token (TTFT). One promising approach to reduce TTFT is to exploit the temporal locality by reusing block-level computations across consecutive frames. Unfortunately, current Transformer-based VLMs break the spatial invariance of CNNs and invalidate the strict-prefix KV-cache mechanism of decoder-only LLMs; in practice, even a single-pixel mismatch can prevent reuse. We present VLMCache, the first system to enable effective block-level visual caching and reuse for on-device VLM inference. The core idea is to disaggregate stable (background) and dynamic (foreground) visual blocks: stable blocks are encoded into the sequence as a reusable KV-cache prefix, while dynamic blocks are recomputed. To make this feasible, VLMCache introduces semantic disaggregation to robustly identify reusable visual blocks and an isolate-then-fuse strategy that restores the global attention interactions and positional coherence lost when foreground and background are separated. Across diverse VLMs and datasets, VLMCache delivers 1.4–3.8× speedup with <1% average accuracy loss, making low-latency VLM inference practical on resource-constrained devices.